rights L124: \nL125: cite90†Rules for challengers L126: \nL127: cite91†Secure and Fair Elections L128: \nL129: cite92†File a Complaint L130: \nL131: # Voting hours\nL132: \nL133: * * *\nL134: \nL135: > Polling places are usually open from 7 a.m. to 8 p.m.\nL136: ## State and federal elections\nL137: \nL138: 7 a.m.* - 8 p.m.\nL139: \nL140: *In state and federal elections, towns with less than 500 residents are not required to open until 10 a.m. but most choose to open at 7 a.m.\nL141: \nL142: cite93†View list of towns with polling places that will open later than 7 a.m. (updated July 2026)\nL143: ## City, town and school-only elections\nL144: \nL145: 7 a.m.* - 8 p.m.\nL146: \nL147: *In elections that are only for cities, towns, and/or school districts, polling places located in the metropolitan area do not have to open until 10 a.m. Those outside the metropolitan area do not have to open until 5 p.m.\nL148: \nL149: The metropolitan area is defined as the counties of Anoka, Carver, Chisago, Dakota, Hennepin, Isanti, Ramsey, Scott, Sherburne, Washington, and Wright.\nL150: Contact your municipal/school clerk or cite8†county elections office to confirm your polling place opening time.\nL151: ## Did you know...?\nL152: \nL153: As long as you're in line by 8 p.m., you can vote, even if you do not reach the front of the line until after 8 p.m.\nL154: ### Elections & Administration\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch Page / Minnesota State Law Library (https://mn.gov/law-library/search/index.jsp?v%3Asources=mn-law-library-opinions)\nciteturn19view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"pattern\":\"Search\"}); Redirected to URL: https://mn.gov/law-library/search/?v:sources=mn-law-library-opinions; Total lines: 232\nL0: cite0†Minnesota State Law Library L1: \nL2: cite1†Image: Minnesota State Law Library printed logo L3: \nL4: [Input]\nL5: \nL6: Search:\nL7: \nL8: [Input: Search] submit\nL9: \nL10: [Button: How do I find?]\nL11:   * cite2†Attorney General Opinions L12:   * cite3†Briefs and Oral Arguments L13:   * cite4†Case Reports by Citation L14:   * cite5†Cases that Interpret a Statute L15:   * cite6†Case Reports by Party Name L16:   * cite7†Case Reports, Free Online Sources L17:   * cite8†Complaints and Other Pleadings L18:   * cite9†Court Records L19:   * cite10†Divorce Decree L20:   * cite11†Information on Changing the Law L21:   * cite12†Judges' Reversal Rates L22:   * cite13†Jury Awards L23:   * cite14†Jury Instructions L24:   * cite15†Legislative History Research†mncourts.libguides.com L25:   * cite16†Minnesota Agency Regulations L26:   * cite17†Older Agency Regulations L27:   * cite18†Older Court Rules L28:   * cite19†Ordinances L29:   * cite20†Paternity Documents L30:   * cite21†Subpoenas L31:   * cite22†Unpublished Decisions L32:   * cite23†Vital Records†mncourts.libguides.com L33:   * cite24†Search Website L34:   * cite25†Search Opinions L35:   * cite26†Browse Opinions by Date L36:   * cite27†Search COA Orders L37:   * cite28†Search Briefs L38:   * cite29†Search Articles L39:   * cite30†Help L40: ## Search Site\nL41: \nL42: [Input] [Input]\nL43: \nL44: Search: [Input: Search]\nL45: \nL46: Sort By:\nL47: \nL48: [Input] Relevance\nL49: \nL50: [Input] Date\nL51: \nL52: [Button: Search] [Input]\nL53: ## Search Opinions\nL54: \nL55: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL56: \nL57: Keyword Search: [Input: Search]\nL58: \nL59: Sort By:\nL60: \nL61: [Input] Relevance\nL62: \nL63: [Input] Date\nL64: \nL65: Hide advanced options\nL66: \nL67: Docket #: Example A14-0123 or ADM14-8123 [Input: ]\nL68: \nL69: Party Names: [Input: ]\nL70: \nL71: Citation:\nL72: [Input: Volume] [Select] [Input: Page]\nL73: Select reporter from the drop-down menu and enter volume and page numbers in the spaces provided. Example: 123 N.W.2d 345. Citation search is available for opinions beginning with 547 N.W.2d (circa 1996).\nL74: \nL75: From Date: [Input: m/d/yyyy]\nL76: To Date: [Input: m/d/yyyy]\nL77: \nL78: Type of Opinion:\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Supreme Court\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Court of Appeals - precedential\nL83: \nL84: [Input] Court of Appeals - nonprecedential\nL85: \nL86: [Input] Court of Appeals - order opinions\nL87: \nL88: About Content\nL89: \nL90: [Button: Search] [Input]\nL91: \nL92: The opinion archive database includes the following content:\nL93:   * Supreme Court opinions and orders (May 2, 1996 - present)\nL94:   * Court of Appeals precedential (published) opinions (May 7, 1996 - present)\nL95:   * Court of Appeals nonprecedential (unpublished) opinions (May 7, 1996 - present)\nL96:   * Court of Appeals - Order opinions (Jan. 2, 2025 - present)\nL97: Precedential Opinions\nL98: Under Minn. Rule of Civil Appellate Procedure 136.01, the Court of Appeals issues a precedential opinion (formerly called \"published opinions\") only to clarify or develop the law. In deciding whether to issue a precedential opinion, the court considers multiple factors listed in Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 136.01, subd.\nL99: (1)(b), including whether the case establishes a new principle or rule of law, clarifies existing case law, decides a novel issue involving a constitutional provision, statute, administrative rule or rule of court, or resolves a significant or recurring legal issue. Precedential opinions will be considered and used by courts faced with similar issues in the future, and they are published in books of caselaw found in most law libraries. Under Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 128.02, subd.\nL100: 1(f), parties may advise the court whether a precedential opinion would be helpful.\nL101: Nonprecedential Opinions\nL102: Nonprecedential opinions (formerly called \"unpublished opinions\") are typically issued in cases that do not contain issues of first impression or involve the application of established law to the particular set of facts and circumstances in that case. See Minn. R. Civ. App. 136.01. Nonprecedential opinions \"are not binding authority except as law of the case, res judicata or collateral estoppel, but nonprecedential opinions may be cited as persuasive authority.\" Minn. R. Civ. App. 136.01.\nL103: Order Opinions\nL104: Order opinions are a simplified format for non-precedential opinions. Cases that involve a few simple issues may be decided by order opinions, which include little discussion of the facts of the case and a brief analysis of the laws that are involved. Order opinions “are not binding authority except as law of the case, res judicata or collateral estoppel.” Minn. R. Civ. App. 136.01.\nL105: The slip opinions published on this website are subject to modification or correction by the court. The official opinions of the Minnesota appellate courts are those published by Thomson West in the North Western Reporter or Minnesota Reporter. Unpublished opinions of the court of appeals are not precedential and may not be cited except as provided by Minn. Stat. 480A.08, subd.3.\nL106: ## Browse Opinions by Date\nL107: ## Special-Term Orders\nL108: \nL109: [Input] [Input]\nL110: \nL111: Keyword Search: [Input: Search]\nL112: \nL113: Sort By:\nL114: \nL115: [Input] Relevance\nL116: \nL117: [Input] Date\nL118: \nL119: [Button: Search] [Input]\nL120: About Content\nL121: This archive includes orders decided by the Court of Appeals at its Special-Term Session, the weekly calendar where a panel of three Court of Appeals judges considers jurisdictional and procedural matters. This archive does not include orders before January 1, 2023 or orders that were not decided at Special Term. Orders not included in this archive may be accessed via the Minnesota Appellate Courts’ public access system, cite31†PMACS†macsnc.courts.state.mn.us .\nL122: ## Search Briefs\nL123: \nL124: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL125: \nL126: Keyword Search: [Input: Search] If you are searching for a brief by docket number, please enter 4 digits after the dash (Example A16-0123 or ADM14-8123).\nL127: \nL128: Citation:\nL129: [Input: Volume] [Select] [Input: Page]\nL130: Select reporter from the drop-down menu and enter volume and page numbers in the spaces provided. Example: 300 N.W.2d 173.\nL131: \nL132: About Content\nL133: \nL134: This database includes:\nL135:   * Appellate briefs filed with the Minnesota Supreme Court in civil and criminal cases that resulted in an opinion.\nL136:   * Appellate briefs filed with the Minnesota Court of Appeals in civil and criminal cases that resulted in a published opinion.\nL137: \nL138: Coverage:\nL139: \nL140:   * 651 NW2d - present.\nL141:   * Briefs from older cases are being added beginning with 300 NW2d.\nL142:   * As of February 2023, we have made it through 340 NW2d.\nL143: Upon request, the Minnesota State Law Library will add briefs not currently available in our archive, unless we are prevented from doing so by applicable court rules or the brief is not available to us. For information about how to obtain briefs not available through this database, please see our cite3†How do I Find Briefs and Oral Arguments page.\nL144: \nL145: [Button: Search] [Input]\nL146: ## Search Articles\nL147: \nL148: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL149: \nL150: Keyword Search: [Input: Search]\nL151: \nL152: Sort By:\nL153: \nL154: [Input] Relevance\nL155: \nL156: [Input] Date\nL157: \nL158: Hide advanced options\nL159: \nL160: Author: (lastname or lastname, firstname) [Input: ]\nL161: \nL162: Subject: (enter keyword or select from list) [Input]\nL163: \nL164: Year (from): [Select]\nL165: \nL166: Year (to): [Select]\nL167: \nL168: Copies of articles can be obtained through the State Law Library. Contact us at askalibrarian@courts.state.mn.us.\nL169: \nL170: cite32†About Content L171: \nL172: [Button: Search] [Input]\nL173: • cite32†https://mn.gov/law-library/research-links/mlpi.jsp L174: • cite33†https://mn.gov/law-library/research-links/mlpipublications.jsp L175: ## Help with searching\nL176: \nL177:   * cite34†Is search case-sensitive? L178: \nL179: No, the search engine is not case-sensitive. For example, a search on \"REPORT\" will also return pages containing \"Report\" or report. A search on \"deed\" will also return pages containing \"DEED\".\nL180: \nL181:   * cite35†Does search check my spelling? L182: Yes, the search engine checks your search words against all of the words collected from the web site. When a word appears to be misspelled, the search engine looks at all the words it knows and offers you a word that could replace it.\nL183: \nL184:   * cite36†Can I search for a phrase? L185: \nL186: To search for a phrase, type the phrase in quotation marks. For example, type \"voter registration\" in quotes in the search box.\nL187: \nL188:   * cite37†Are Boolean searching and wildcards allowed? L189:     * Boolean operators must be typed in UPPER CASE or they will not be recognized as operators.\nL190:     * The Boolean operators 'OR', 'NOT', '-', and '+' may be used in your query.\nL191:     * Using 'AND' is not necessary since all words are searched by default.\nL192:     * The asterisk '*' can be used as a wildcard character.\nL193: Boolean Operator  | Description\nL194: OR  | Use 'OR' when you require only one of the search words to appear in the results. Example: \"city OR county\"\nL195: NOT or -  | Use 'NOT' or '-' when you do not want to exclude results that contain a particular term. Examples: \"economic development NOT support\" or \" economic development -support \"\nL196: *  | Use '*' as a wildcard to search on all words that contain the letters you enter. You must enter a minimum of three letters plus the '*' character. Example: \"employ*\" would find \"employ\" or \"employer\" or \"employee\"\nL197: +  | Use '+' when you require the exact word to appear in all of the results. For multiple words, you must use put a + sign in front of each word that must be exact. Example: search on \"+lipitor cholesterol\" if you only want to retrieve results with the brand name \"Lipitor\" and the word \"cholesterol\", and no results with the generic synonym \"Atorvastatin\"\nL198:   * cite38†What do I do if I get a message saying, \"No results were found for your query\"? L199: \nL200: There are a few reasons why you could have received this message:\nL201: \nL202:     * Your word may be misspelled and the search engine can't find a substitute for it. For example, if you had typed in \"metakare\" when you meant \"medicare\", the search engine won't be able to recognize it.\nL203: \nL204:     * The information you are looking for may not be available on this website.\nL205: \nL206:   * cite39†What types of files are searched? "
